---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { withBase } from '@lib/paths';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = withBase(`/tool/${category.segment}/${config.slug}/`);
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>

  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>Quick Answer <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      This gratuity calculator works out the tip amount, the total bill after tip, and the
      per-person cost for bill splitting. Enter the bill, tip percentage, and number of
      people. On a $60 bill with a 20% tip split three ways: tip = $12, total = $72, per
      person = $24. Tip calculation runs instantly with no account required.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Try the Tip Calculator →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="how-much-to-tip">
    <h2>How Much Should You Tip? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      In the United States, standard restaurant tips are 15-20% for good service. Excellent
      service warrants 20-25%. Counter service and takeout orders receive 10-15%.
      Delivery drivers get 15-20% on the food subtotal, with a minimum of $3-5 for small
      orders since the fixed cost of a trip does not scale with order size.
    </p>
    <p>
      For a sit-down restaurant: a $45 bill at 20% tip = $9 tip, $54 total. For a $120
      group dinner at 18% = $21.60 tip, $141.60 total. At a coffee counter for a $6 drink:
      $0.60-$0.90 at 10-15%. The calculation is the same at every price point.
    </p>
    <p>
      Other services where tipping is standard in the US: hair and nail salons (15-20%),
      taxi and rideshare (15-20%), hotel housekeeping ($2-5 per night), movers (5-10% of the
      total bill), tour guides ($5-15 per person). These are conventions, not laws; adjust
      based on service quality.
    </p>
    <p>
      Tipping customs differ significantly outside the US. Japan: tipping is not customary and
      can be considered rude. Most of Europe: a small tip or rounding up is appreciated but
      not required, and service charges appear on many bills automatically. Australia and New
      Zealand: tipping is not expected but welcomed. Always check local customs before
      traveling to avoid awkward exchanges.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="note" heading="Check for automatic gratuity on the bill">
      <p>Many restaurants add an automatic gratuity of 18-20% for parties of 6 or more. If a service charge already appears on your bill, you do not need to add a further tip. Adding a second tip on top of automatic gratuity is a common and expensive mistake.</p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="how-it-works">
    <h2>How Does a Tip Calculator Work? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      This gratuity tool takes three inputs: bill amount, tip percentage, and number of
      people. It computes the tip as (Bill × Percentage) ÷ 100, adds it to the bill for the
      grand total, then divides the grand total by the number of people for the per-person
      share. As a bill splitter, it always divides the full total (bill plus tip), not just
      the pre-tip bill.
    </p>
    <p>
      The fastest mental method for 20%: move the decimal one place left to find 10%, then
      double it. On a $47 bill: 10% = $4.70; 20% = $9.40. Round up to $9.50 if service
      was good.
    </p>
    <p>
      For 15%: find 10% and add half. On a $47 bill: 10% = $4.70; half of that is $2.35;
      15% = $7.05. Round to $7.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="splitting-the-bill">
    <h2>How Do You Split the Bill Evenly? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Add the tip to the bill total first, then divide by the number of people. On a $90 bill
      with a $18 tip (20%): grand total = $108; split among 6 people = $18 each.
    </p>
    <p>
      A common mistake is splitting the pre-tip bill, then asking each person to add their
      share of the tip separately. This works but creates friction. Adding the tip first and
      splitting the grand total is cleaner: everyone pays one number.
    </p>
    <p>
      For uneven splits, where people ordered different amounts, calculate each person's
      fraction of the pre-tip subtotal and apply that fraction to the tip as well. The tip
      calculator handles even splits automatically.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="key-idea" heading="Split the total, not just the bill">
      <p>Always divide the bill-plus-tip total by the number of diners. Splitting only the food bill and handling the tip separately leads to underpayment or confusion about who covers what.</p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="pre-tax-vs-post-tax">
    <h2>Does the Tip Apply Before or After Tax? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      A tip percentage applies to the bill before tax is added, not the total with tax already
      included. The server provided the service at the pre-tax price; the tax is a government
      charge. Standard advice is to base tip calculation on the pre-tax subtotal.
    </p>
    <p>
      On a $100 pre-tax bill with 10% sales tax ($10 tax): pre-tax 20% tip = $20; post-tax
      20% tip = $22. The gap is $2. For everyday restaurant visits, the difference is small
      and rounding up to the post-tax total is common. For large catered events at $2,000 pre-tax, the
      $40 difference becomes meaningful.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="examples">
    <h2>Tip Calculation Examples <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>Common scenarios with worked numbers. For example:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Two people, dinner.</strong> Bill: $74. 20% tip: $14.80. Total: $88.80.
        Split two ways: $44.40 each.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Group of five, restaurant.</strong> Bill: $135. 18% tip: $24.30.
        Total: $159.30. Per person: $31.86.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Delivery order.</strong> Food subtotal: $28. 20% tip: $5.60.
        Total including tip: $33.60. (Delivery fees listed separately do not replace the tip.)
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Comped item.</strong> Original bill: $80, one item comped leaving $55 charged.
        Tip on full $80 pre-discount: $16. Paying $55 plus $16 tip = $71 total.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="common-mistakes">
    <h2>Common Mistakes <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>

    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Tipping on the total when gratuity is already included">
      <p>
        Check the bill before tipping. Many restaurants add an automatic gratuity (18-20%)
        for large parties or during special events. If a service charge appears as a line
        item, it is already a tip. Adding another tip on top doubles the gratuity without
        any benefit to the server beyond the standard amount.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>

    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Splitting only the food and forgetting the tip in the total">
      <p>
        If four people split a $80 bill equally at $20 each, then each needs to add their
        share of the tip. A 20% tip is $16 total, so $4 per person, making the actual cost
        $24 per person. Collecting $20 each and leaving a $16 tip on top requires someone
        to cover the overage or leaves a short payment.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="related-tools">
    <h2>Related Tools <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href={withBase('/tool/number/percentage-calculator/')}>Percentage Calculator</a>: Calculate any percentage, useful for custom tip amounts outside the standard presets.</li>
      <li><a href={withBase('/tool/number/discount-calculator/')}>Discount Calculator</a>: Find the final price after a discount, helpful when tipping on a comped or discounted meal.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

</GuideLayout>

<style>
  .cta-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    height: var(--touch-target);
    padding-inline: var(--space-5);
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
    background: var(--color-accent);
    color: var(--color-text-inverse);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: var(--transition-color);
  }

  .cta-link:hover {
    opacity: 0.9;
    text-decoration: none;
  }
</style>
